    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Yordan Alvarez** standing as our undisputed anchor, boasting an elite score of 69.07 against Matthews. With a blistering .423 xwOBA and a prime #3 spot in the order, he is the clear centerpiece of the offense. We are also leaning into the speed-first profile of **Caleb Durbin**, whose score of 59.28 is inflated by a favorable matchup against Falter (5.14 xERA). While hitting at the bottom of the order carries a penalty, the SP skill advantage here is too significant to ignore in a format where stolen bases remain our primary scarcity target. **Casey Schmitt** also presents a strong play (58.86) due to his dynamic platoon advantage against Nelson, while **Christian Yelich** (57.83) gets a slight boost from Brown\u2019s slow delivery, which historically opens up higher-percentage swipe opportunities.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved the exclusion of **Colt Keith**, who remains sidelined by lineup rotation decisions despite a base projection that suggests he could provide value in the right platoon context. He was the most viable candidate to displace **Dillon Dingler** (49.32) or **Carson Benge** (46.96), but both maintained their starting spots due to their specific order positioning and positional necessity. We remain cautious regarding **Trea Turner** (52.91) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (54.71), as both are facing elite arms in Burns and Misiorowski, respectively; their scores are heavily suppressed by the SP skill factor, necessitating a close watch on their early-game at-bats to ensure they don't drag down our daily efficiency. Meanwhile, **Zac Veen** and **Lars Nootbaar** remain non-factors on the bench due to minor league assignments and IL designations, respectively, leaving us thin on depth should any late-inning defensive replacements or weather delays disrupt the current lineup.",
